WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.121
Here's a small B+tree.

2
00:00:04.121 --> 00:00:08.705
Each node is one page, and in this toy a page holds at most three keys.

3
00:00:08.705 --> 00:00:14.581
The root's signposts send each search to one leaf, and the leaves hold every key, in order.

4
00:00:14.733 --> 00:00:16.181
Let's insert 100.

5
00:00:16.181 --> 00:00:18.651
An insert starts as a search.

6
00:00:18.651 --> 00:00:26.913
At the root, 100 isn't below 30, 50 or 70, so we follow the last pointer, to the leaf 70, 80, 90.

7
00:00:27.067 --> 00:00:28.515
But that leaf is full.

8
00:00:28.515 --> 00:00:29.701
Where does 100 go?

9
00:00:29.701 --> 00:00:32.862
And while we're at it: this tree has two levels.

10
00:00:32.862 --> 00:00:34.837
How would it ever get a third?

11
00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:37.740
100 goes into the leaf in key order.

12
00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:41.549
Now the leaf has four keys, and a node may hold three.

13
00:00:41.549 --> 00:00:45.076
On a real page, the new entry doesn't fit in 8 KB.

14
00:00:45.267 --> 00:00:47.797
The first idea: make the page bigger.

15
00:00:47.797 --> 00:00:49.712
But pages have a fixed size.

16
00:00:49.712 --> 00:00:51.900
That's the whole point of pages.

17
00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:56.683
The second idea: hang an extra page off the full leaf, an overflow page.

18
00:00:56.683 --> 00:01:01.965
Every key that lands there piles into the chain, which has to be read page by page.

19
00:01:01.965 --> 00:01:05.211
That's the slow search we built the tree to escape.

20
00:01:05.211 --> 00:01:10.367
It's a full shelf with a cardboard box next to it, then another box next to that.

21
00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:13.178
Think of the library from the last section.

22
00:01:13.178 --> 00:01:20.036
The board by the door says which floor, a sign on each floor says which shelf, and each shelf keeps its books in order.

23
00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:31.279
When a shelf is full, the librarian puts a new shelf right next to it, moves the upper half of the books onto it, and adds one line to the floor sign: from here on, the new shelf.

24
00:01:31.467 --> 00:01:37.959
So the shelves are the leaves, the floor signs are the internal nodes, and the board by the door is the root.

25
00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:40.063
Let's do that to our leaf.

26
00:01:40.063 --> 00:01:45.421
A new leaf appears on its right, and the upper half, 90 and 100, moves into it.

27
00:01:45.421 --> 00:01:48.133
The two leaves are linked, side by side.

28
00:01:48.333 --> 00:01:53.664
Now the parent needs a pointer to the new leaf, and a signpost that says where it starts.

29
00:01:53.664 --> 00:01:56.419
Which key goes up, and does it leave the leaf?

30
00:01:56.419 --> 00:01:57.377
Pause and guess.

31
00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:02.636
90, the new leaf's first key.

32
00:02:02.636 --> 00:02:08.883
And it's copied, not moved: 90 stays in the leaf, because the leaves must hold every key.

33
00:02:08.883 --> 00:02:10.007
This is a split.

34
00:02:10.367 --> 00:02:13.421
But now the root has four keys, one too many.

35
00:02:13.421 --> 00:02:16.000
So it splits too, with one difference.

36
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:21.363
Its keys are only signposts, so the middle key, 70, doesn't need to stay below.

37
00:02:21.363 --> 00:02:24.213
It's pushed up, and it leaves both halves.

38
00:02:24.213 --> 00:02:26.250
30 and 50 keep three children.

39
00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:27.132
90 keeps two.

40
00:02:27.167 --> 00:02:31.087
And the node that split was the root, so 70 has nowhere to go.

41
00:02:31.087 --> 00:02:34.817
A new root is made above it, with one key and two children.

42
00:02:35.167 --> 00:02:40.062
That's the only way a B+tree gets taller: at the top, never at the bottom.

43
00:02:40.062 --> 00:02:46.081
So every leaf is always at the same depth, and every search reads the same number of pages.

44
00:02:46.267 --> 00:02:53.847
A search for 60 now reads three: the root says below 70, the next node says at least 50, and the leaf holds it.

45
00:02:54.033 --> 00:02:56.026
Here the library picture breaks.

46
00:02:56.026 --> 00:02:58.889
A library grows at its edges, with a new wing.

47
00:02:58.889 --> 00:03:07.045
This tree grows by putting a new sign above the board at the door, so every shelf stays the same number of signs from the entrance.

48
00:03:07.433 --> 00:03:12.448
On a real PostgreSQL page, a leaf holds 407 of these entries.

49
00:03:12.448 --> 00:03:18.120
In the Try it, keys 1 to 407 fit in one leaf, which is also the root.

50
00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:27.656
Key 408 splits it, and the tree grows a level: a new root on top, one leaf with 367 entries, and a new leaf with 42.

51
00:03:27.833 --> 00:03:28.758
Why so uneven?

52
00:03:28.758 --> 00:03:31.530
The leaf that split was the rightmost one.

53
00:03:31.530 --> 00:03:39.651
When every new key is bigger than the last, PostgreSQL keeps the left page 90% full, because nothing will land there again.

54
00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:44.081
So the kind of key decides how full your pages are.

55
00:03:44.081 --> 00:03:50.323
Random keys split pages in the middle, and pages settle about two-thirds full.

56
00:03:50.323 --> 00:03:54.804
In the Try it, 200,000 random ids took 1,038 leaf pages.

57
00:03:54.804 --> 00:03:59.766
200,000 time-ordered ids, each bigger than the last, took 767.

58
00:03:59.766 --> 00:04:03.287
That's a third more pages for the same rows.

59
00:04:03.467 --> 00:04:10.087
And each split writes three pages, the old leaf, the new leaf and the parent, and logs every change first.

60
00:04:10.467 --> 00:04:13.594
So: a full leaf splits and copies its first key up.

61
00:04:13.594 --> 00:04:17.090
A full internal node splits and pushes its middle key up.

62
00:04:17.090 --> 00:04:20.279
And when the root splits, the tree grows at the top.

63
00:04:20.733 --> 00:04:22.023
One quick check.

64
00:04:22.023 --> 00:04:24.280
Insert 85 into the new tree.

65
00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:25.892
Does anything split?

66
00:04:25.892 --> 00:04:27.181
Pause and think.

67
00:04:30.433 --> 00:04:30.691
No.

68
00:04:30.691 --> 00:04:37.653
The root sends 85 right, the node 90 sends it left, and the leaf 70, 80 has room.

69
00:04:37.653 --> 00:04:39.115
One page changes.
